<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tuvalu</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tuvalu is an archipelagic country in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polynesia">Polynesia</a>, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pacific-ocean">Pacific Ocean</a> midway between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawaii">Hawaii</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>, made up of three reef islands and six atolls. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polynesians">Polynesians</a> were its first inhabitants, spreading out from Samoa and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tonga">Tonga</a>. Álvaro de Mendaña was the first European known to sail through the archipelago, in 1568. A British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> was proclaimed in 1892, and the islands joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/humphrey-gilbert">Gilbert</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ellice-islands">Ellice Islands</a> Colony in 1916. After a referendum in 1974 the colony was divided, and Tuvalu became a separate colony on 1 January 1976. It became fully independent on 1 October 1978.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Tuvalu consists of three reef islands, Nanumanga, Niutao and Niulakita, and six true atolls, Funafuti, Nanumea, Nui, Nukufetau, Nukulaelae and Vaitupu, with a land area of only about 26 square kilometres. The islands lie west of the International Date Line and have poor soil. The highest point, on Niulakita, is 4.6 metres above sea level, and the islands are open to flooding in cyclones and storms. Rainwater harvesting is the principal source of fresh water. The name means eight standing together in Tuvaluan, because eight of the nine islands were inhabited.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The first inhabitants were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polynesians">Polynesians</a>, part of a migration into the Pacific that began about 3,000 years ago, and there was frequent canoe voyaging between the nearer islands, including Samoa and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tonga">Tonga</a>. Álvaro de Mendaña sighted the islands on 16 January 1568, sailing past Nui, which he charted as Isla de Jesús because the previous day was the feast of the Holy Name. He made contact with the islanders but could not land.</p><p>Trading companies became active in the mid-19th century. John O'Brien was the first European to settle, as a trader on Funafuti in the 1850s, and he married Salai, the daughter of the paramount chief. British subjects in the islands came under the Western Pacific High Commission in 1877, and Captain Herbert Gibson of HMS Curacoa declared <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorates</a> over each island between 9 and 16 October 1892. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states-marine-corps">United States Marine Corps</a> landed on Funafuti on 2 October 1942, and the atoll became a base for attacks on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilbert-islands">Gilbert Islands</a>, occupied by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>. Toaripi Lauti became the first prime minister on 1 October 1978, and Tuvalu joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> on 5 September 2000 as its 189th member.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Funafuti is the capital. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> of 12 December 2022 recorded 10,643 people, and Tuvaluan and English are the national languages. The Congregational Christian Church of Tuvalu is the state church, which in practice gives it the privilege of conducting special services on national occasions. Each island has its own high chief, called ulu-aliki, several sub-chiefs and a community council, the Falekaupule, the traditional assembly of elders. The country is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitutional-monarchy">constitutional monarchy</a> within the Commonwealth with no regular military forces.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tuvalu” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
